19th century london its gas street lamps  thick fog  pollution and claustrophobic streets  inspired histories and legends  the largest urban environment in the  world at the time  london boasted rapid industrialization  significant job opportunities  brutal class division crowded slums  and a fire-breathing wall-leaping  claw-wielding devil  who attacked women in their homes and in  the streets terrorizing a community for  some  67 years introducing one of the world's  first  urban legends his name was springhill  jack  i'm dr emily zarka and this is monstrum  early in the 1800s reports of a leaping  man in the form of a ghostly apparition  had already emerged in folklore in  rumors  but the flesh and blood creature took  form in january 1826  when the northampton mercury reported  that a masked man with spring boots  which enable him to vault over a 10 feet  wall was spotted  a man jumping walls in the middle of the  night can easily be dismissed as a prank  right maybe at first but in 1837 his  behavior  and his notoriety changed that year  multiple women gave statements to the  police that they'd been violently  attacked by a monster  who would ring their doorbell or accost  them in the street and then  tear their clothes with his claws since  the victims were working class women  early reports weren't taken as seriously  at first  that is until in january 1838 the lord  mayor of the city of london  sir john cowan read a letter to the  press outside his mansion  the anonymous author claimed they wanted  to bring reports of a supernatural  attacker to the attention of the  authorities  he believed the letters authored to be a  woman who had become  senseless as a result of the strange  attacker and mockingly implored her to  write a description of the demon  that would enable him to catch him now  that the mysterious jack had been  discussed by the mayor  a male authority figure of the upper  class the real attention began  a few days later headlines blamed spring  jack for the attacks  the west kent guardian finally gave the  unknown perpetrator  a title that stuck spring-heeled jack  with the papers giving more pages to the  figure rumors and stories of his attack  spread  wildly and londoners jumped at shadows  convinced springhill jack was coming for  them next  children and adults alike told stories  about him  then in february 20th 1838 miss jane  alsop  the 18 year old daughter of a gentleman  heard a ringing at her gate and there  discovered a man claiming to be a  policeman he announced that they had  captured spring hill jack and she needed  to bring the officer a candle  as jane did so the man threw off his  cloak and applying the lighted candle to  his breast  presented a most hideous and frightful  appearance and vomited  forth a quantity of blue and white flame  from his mouth  and his eyes resembled red balls of fire  he placed her head under one of his arms  and commenced tearing her gown with his  claws  which she believed to be made of  something metallic with great violence  he tore at her neck and arms and even  removed a quantity of hair from her head  one of her sisters heard her screaming  and saw the figure dragging jane down  the stone steps of the house  she barely managed to pull jane back  inside and slam the door  at this time her sister's dress was  nearly torn off  both her combs dragged out of her head  the man continued to knock on the door  until they called loudly for the police  another similar attack by a man of tall  thin and gentlemanly appearance  wearing a cloak appeared in papers a  little over a week later  very quickly springhill jack was labeled  an aristocrat  a report on february 27 1838 in the  morning herald  said that a young servant boy opened the  door to find someone of hideous  appearance  with a claw-like hand and clothing  embroidered with a family crest with the  letter w  a family crest the letter w are first  clues as to the identity of this leaping  legend  an aristocrat maybe someone already  notorious  one man in particular was given credit  for inspiring the first spring heel jack  legends  the marquis of waterford born in april  1811 the young henry de la por beresford  became the third marquis of waterford  following the death of his parents and  older brother educated at eaton college  his penchant for mischief and drinking  earned him a reputation in the press  long before spring healed jack leapt  into the scene reports of the young  marquis smashing windows  offering money to people to fight him  throwing meat out of a butcher shop  and accosting people while naked had  appeared in the press  and he was arrested multiple times for  drunk and disorderly conduct  in one notable incident in april 1837  after our phantom leaper appeared on the  landscape but before  spring healed jack had become a legend  the newspaper the champion says the  marquee and his friends were charged  with starting a riot  and subsequently appeared before the  court wearing bare-skinned coats and  large cravats  reports of the young lords exploits  served as regular fodder for the  newspapers in 1837  the same year we see spring heal jack  described as a devil-like  gentleman as stories of the scandalous  marquis circulated they may have swirled  with the emerging persona of  spring-heeled jack  and it could be in this way the marquee  morphed into a sort of scapegoat  londoners could blame for any evil or  inconvenience  or maybe he was to blame for the crimes  it is certainly possible that the  victims of spring healed jack who  reported being physically assaulted by a  man with claws  were really attacked by human male  assailants but chose to blame the  faceless spring-heeled jack rather than  someone they knew  newspapers at the time at first believed  that attacks to be committed by one  individual  but as reports increased many proposed  it was likely copycats of the original  perpetrator  there were many arrests of men like  charles grenville and james painter  said to be imitating springhill jack  frightening women and children  things turned murderous in 1845 when  maria davis was attacked in daylight it  was reported that springhill jack spit  blue and white flame in her face  then threw her over a bridge into an  open sewer where she drowned  however this account cannot be verified  by any existing records or death  certificates  if this murder never happened then why  suddenly make jack a killer  perhaps the story is meant to further  scare young women about the dangers of  unfamiliar men  springhill jack's character can be  attributed to the genres in which his  stories were published  legitimate newspapers reported his  attacks as hard news  but sensationalized fiction stories  depicted him as an avenger of crime  it was hard for people to tell if he was  a real threat or not  victorian london was a brutal place in  both reality and fiction  people were looking for entertainment  and they seemed to like it best served  with a healthy dose of violence  a play based on spring heel jack called  spring heel jack  premiered on february 26 1838 billed as  a melodrama the creature was brought to  the stage at the royal pavilion theater  which catered to the working class in  1863  a 40-part penny dreadful a type of pulp  fiction story meant to titillate and  shock a generally working-class audience  cast jack as a hero avenging women who  suffered injustice and oppression  the masked version of jack was a tall  form whose head and body glowed  with a blue phosphorescent fire from the  back of which hung  in graceful folds a long striped cloak  multiple times throughout the series the  removal of jack's mask revealed his  identity to the characters in the story  who recognize him  but the name is always cut off midward  as the mark  likely a reference to the marquee quite  possibly the then deceased beresford  newspapers claim springhill jack is a  terrifying boogeyman who scares people  to death  or even outright murders them but in the  penny dreadful series he's a prankster  that functions as a type of robin hood  type figure  for the disenfranchised in one example  he terrifies a girl with his strange and  horrible face with fang-like teeth  and vomit smoke in her face causing her  to faint  only after doing this he writes her a  note saying that he tried to save her  father from being murdered earlier in  the evening  and promises to avenge his death then he  scratches a double cross on her left  shoulder  which is a confusing sequence of events  but hey penny dreadfuls were weird  springhill jack reached such a level of  notoriety that he became the first  victorian urban legend  everyone either claimed to have  encountered him or claimed they knew  someone who had  an urban legend is defined as an  entertaining but usually horrifying  story  based on hearsay that is nevertheless  widely circulated as  true but in reality the majority of  spring heal jack's attacks were likely  false but that's what an urban legend is  all about it's about the friend of a  friend or  my cousin's roommate's boyfriend who had  something bad happen to them  in 19th century london a time and place  of scientific advancement and  technological development  but also abject poverty and income  inequality  springhill jack gave the people  something to hold on to a mystery that  could not be solved by mere  science or even solid police work his  custom footwear constituted a  technological marvel  yet the paranormal components to his  story nodded to age-old superstitions  unlike other monsters of the time spring  hill jack was homegrown  prowling the streets where they lived  not off in some foreign country or  remote location  he was local marvelous fear-inducing  inspiring  springfield jack manifested the fears of  the development of urban spaces  and the upheaval of social classes and  lifestyle changes  city life afforded some scholars argued  that victorian england seemingly  insatiable desire for performative  violence was rooted in the need to keep  people from acting on their real needs  for violence  figures like springhill jack may have  served as an emotional catharsis  perhaps this is also why so many of the  monstrous creatures in this century  appear human or are human frankenstein's  creature  vampires sweeney todd mummies mr hyde  dorian gray the list goes on and of  course there was the very real monster  whose brutal crimes quickly came to  overshadow spring healed jack  jack the ripper this makes me think  about why we've seen spring heel jack  appear  again in popular culture most recently  he's appeared in a comic series a  graphic novel and an award-winning book  part of this comes from the increased  popularity of stories set in highly  stylized victorian settings  like steampunk but part of me wonders if  like the victorians  we too are searching for an outlet for  our own dark desires  finally in this representative  pseudo-historical manned beast whose  story is set in the industrial  revolution  and thus the birth of the modern world  we see another kind of birth  that of the urban legend you're welcome  bloody mary and sewer alligator  sir johan sir john cohen did i say her  say  herstory
 

